    "title": "Menendez Brothers Begin Parole Hearings After Nearly 30 Years in Prison",
    "text": "• Erik and Lyle Menendez will present their cases for parole starting Thursday.\n• The brothers were convicted in 1996 for the 1989 murders of their parents, Jose and Kitty Menendez.\n• They were sentenced to life in prison without the possibility of parole.\n• In May, a Los Angeles judge reduced their sentences to 50 years to life, making them eligible for parole.\n• The brothers were under 26 years old when they committed their crimes, allowing for parole eligibility under California law.\n• Erik Menendez's hearing is scheduled for Thursday morning, followed by Lyle Menendez on Friday.\n• The hearings will be conducted via videoconference from prison in San Diego.\n• The parole board will evaluate the brothers based on their criminal history, motivation for the crime, signs of remorse, behavior in prison, and future plans.\n• If granted parole, the chief legal counsel has 120 days to review the case, followed by a 30-day period for Governor Gavin Newsom to affirm or deny the parole.\n• Governor Newsom has not indicated whether he will support the parole request.\n• The Menendez brothers have family support, with relatives expected to speak at the hearings.\n• The case has gained significant media attention, including documentaries and dramatizations.\n• LA County District Attorney Nathan Hochman opposes parole, citing a lack of full insight into their crimes.\n• Recent rules violations, including possession of cellphones, could negatively impact their parole chances.\n• Parole decisions are ultimately made by individual commissioners, who may consider various factors, including celebrity status and family support.\n• One media representative has been selected to view the proceedings virtually and report to the press.",
